(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-peaa"]={"plain":{"bills":{"peaa":{"slug":"peaa","title":"The Keep Your Benefits Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the *\"Keep Your Benefits Act\"*.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n**(1)** Federal, State, and local programs that condition a benefit on income require an applicant to establish that income to each program separately. The same individual establishes the same fact repeatedly, in different forms, on different cycles, to different agencies.\n\n**(2)** The information necessary to establish that fact is, in the ordinary case, already in the possession of the Federal Government. Employers report wages on information returns and report quarterly wage data to State agencies; State agencies report unemployment compensation claims; and these reports are already collected, transmitted, and retained under existing law.\n\n**(3)** An individual whose income is below the threshold requiring a Federal income tax return is not required to file one and commonly does not. Such an individual therefore has no established income record, notwithstanding that the Federal Government holds the reports described in paragraph (2).\n\n**(4)** The consequence falls hardest on those entitled to the most. In each of tax years 2019 through 2021, between 3,500,000 and 5,700,000 tax units eligible for the earned income tax credit did not claim it, leaving between $4,700,000,000 and $8,200,000,000 unclaimed, and most of those tax units filed no return.\n\n**(5)** Section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 already authorizes disclosure of return information to Federal, State, and local agencies administering enumerated benefit programs, for the purpose of determining eligibility for, or the correct amount of, benefits. That authority operates on written request in an individual case, and its list of covered programs is fixed by enumeration.\n\n**(6)** Inaccuracy in program eligibility runs in two directions. A benefit not received by a person entitled to it and a payment made in error are both failures of accuracy, and both are reduced by verification against information the Government already holds.\n\n**(7)** Programs define the unit whose income is counted in materially different ways. A record that reported a household rather than the facts from which a household is composed would be accurate as to no program and misleading as to several."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a) Verified income record.**\u2014The term *\"verified income record\"* means the record compiled under section 1-1 with respect to an individual. **The term does not mean a determination of eligibility for any benefit, and does not include any conclusion as to whether an individual satisfies any requirement of any program.**\n\n**(b) Composition fact.**\u2014The term *\"composition fact\"* means a fact appearing in information described in section 1-2 concerning the relationship of one individual to another, including whether an individual was claimed as a dependent by another individual, whether individuals filed jointly, and whether individuals share an address of record. **The term does not include any characterization of those facts as constituting a household, family, assistance unit, or other unit of any program.**\n\n**(c) Covered program.**\u2014The term *\"covered program\"* means a program administered by a Federal, State, local, or Tribal agency that conditions eligibility for, or the amount of, a benefit on the income of an individual, and that has elected in under section 3-1.\n\n**(d) Direction.**\u2014The term *\"direction\"* means an instruction given by an individual, in a form the Secretary shall prescribe, that a verified income record be furnished to a covered program identified by the individual. **An application to a covered program is a direction as to that program.** **Until a form is prescribed, a writing by the individual identifying the individual and the covered program suffices; the absence of a prescribed form defeats no direction.**\n\n**(e) Furnish.**\u2014The term *\"furnish\"* means to transmit a verified income record to the individual to whom it relates or, on that individual's direction, to a covered program.\n\n**(f) Information return.**\u2014The term *\"information return\"* means a return, statement, or other document required to be filed with the Secretary reporting amounts paid to or on behalf of an individual.\n\n**(g) Quarterly wage data.**\u2014The term *\"quarterly wage data\"* means the wage and unemployment compensation information supplied under section 453A(g)(2) of the Social Security Act and maintained in the National Directory of New Hires established under section 453(i) of that Act. **The term includes only information that has not been deleted from that directory under section 453(i)(2)(A) of that Act.**\n\n**(h) Individual.**\u2014The term *\"individual\"* means a natural person with respect to whom the Secretary holds information described in section 1-2. **An individual need not have filed a return of Federal income tax for any period.**\n\n**(i) Secretary.**\u2014The term *\"Secretary\"* means the Secretary of the Treasury.\n\n**(j) Benefit.**\u2014The term *\"benefit\"* includes a payment, coverage, subsidy, allowance, service, waiver, or reduction of a charge, however denominated, provided under a program, and a credit against tax whose amount or availability depends on the income of an individual."},{"anchor":"sec-0-4","heading":"\u00a7 0-4. Rules of construction.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) No eligibility determined.**\u2014Nothing in this Act authorizes the Secretary, or any agency receiving a verified income record, to treat that record as determining eligibility for any benefit. **Each covered program applies its own eligibility rules to the facts stated in the record.**\n\n**(b) No entitlement created.**\u2014This Act creates no entitlement to any benefit and modifies the eligibility rules of no program.\n\n**(c) No new collection authorized.**\u2014**This Act authorizes the collection of no information.** Every input to a verified income record is information required to be reported under a provision of law in effect on the date of enactment of this Act, and nothing in this Act shall be construed to authorize the Secretary or any other agency to require, purchase, or otherwise acquire any information not so required.\n\n**(d) Construction of the title.**\u2014The title of this Act refers to the accuracy of determinations made by covered programs. **It does not confer, and shall not be construed to confer, authority to make such determinations.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Establishment.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014The Secretary shall compile, with respect to each individual, a verified income record.\n\n**(b) Without application.**\u2014A verified income record shall be compiled **without any application, request, election, or other action by the individual**, and whether or not the individual has filed a return of Federal income tax for any period.\n\n**(c) Recompilation.**\u2014The Secretary shall recompile each verified income record on the cycle provided in section 5-1.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Sources.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) Permitted sources.**\u2014A verified income record shall be compiled from, and only from\u2014\n\n**(1)** information returns;\n\n**(2)** returns of Federal income tax filed by or with respect to the individual;\n\n**(3)** quarterly wage data; and\n\n**(4)** information supplied by the individual under section 1-5.\n\n**(b) No other source.**\u2014**The Secretary shall not compile any part of a verified income record from any source not listed in subsection (a).** The omission of a source from that list is a determination of Congress that it shall not be used, and not a gap to be filled by regulation, agreement, or practice.\n\n**(c) Limits carried forward.**\u2014Quarterly wage data may be used under subsection (a)(3) **only to the extent, and for the periods, that it remains available under section 453(i)(2)(A) of the Social Security Act.** Nothing in this Act extends the period for which that information is retained, alters the conditions on its use under section 453(j) of that Act, or authorizes its use for any purpose other than compiling a verified income record.\n\n**(d) Gaps stated, not filled.**\u2014Where a source described in subsection (a) does not cover a period, **the record shall so state under section 1-3(a)(5). The Secretary shall not estimate, impute, extrapolate, or otherwise supply the missing information.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Contents.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) Required contents.**\u2014A verified income record shall state\u2014\n\n**(1)** the income of the individual, by source and by period, as shown in the information described in section 1-2;\n\n**(2)** each composition fact appearing in that information;\n\n**(3)** the period to which the record relates;\n\n**(4)** the date on which the record was compiled and the date of the most recent information reflected in it; and\n\n**(5)** where information for a period is absent, **that it is absent**, and the period for which it is absent.\n\n**(b) Facts only.**\u2014A verified income record shall state facts appearing in the information described in section 1-2. **It shall not state a conclusion, characterization, estimate, or projection, and shall not assign the individual to any unit, category, or classification of any program.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Effect.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) Not a determination.**\u2014A verified income record is not a determination of eligibility for any benefit, is not a finding, and binds no agency as to any question of eligibility.\n\n**(b) Weight.**\u2014A covered program may rely on a verified income record as evidence of the facts it states. **Nothing in this Act requires a covered program to accept a verified income record as conclusive, or prevents an individual from establishing a fact by other means.**\n\n**(c) Adverse use foreclosed.**\u2014**No agency may use a verified income record as a ground for denying, reducing, suspending, or terminating a benefit except by applying its own eligibility rules to the facts stated in the record.** **The absence of a verified income record is not a ground for any adverse action and supports no inference as to any fact the record would have stated.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Correction.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) Right to correct.**\u2014An individual may at any time furnish the Secretary information correcting or supplementing the individual's verified income record, in a form the Secretary shall prescribe. **Until a form is prescribed, a writing suffices, and the absence of a prescribed form defeats no correction.**\n\n**(b) Effect of correction.**\u2014Information furnished under subsection (a) shall be reflected in the record, identified as supplied by the individual, and shall not displace information described in section 1-2(a)(1) through (3) but shall appear alongside it.\n\n**(c) Ministerial.**\u2014Reflecting information furnished under subsection (a) is a ministerial duty."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Furnishing; failure to furnish.","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 THE VERIFIED INCOME RECORD","body":"**(a) Duty.**\u2014On the direction of an individual, the Secretary shall furnish that individual's verified income record to the covered program identified, not later than the period specified in the SCHEDULE. **This duty is ministerial and is enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by the individual to whom the record relates.**\n\n**(b) Failure to furnish.**\u2014**Where the Secretary has not furnished a verified income record within the period specified in the SCHEDULE, the covered program shall accept the individual's own written attestation of the facts the record would have stated, and shall determine eligibility upon it, until the record is furnished.**\n\n**(c) Reconciliation.**\u2014On furnishing of the record, the covered program shall reconcile any benefit determined on an attestation under subsection (b) to the facts stated in the record. **Such reconciliation may increase a benefit and may adjust a benefit prospectively. It shall not give rise to a recovery of any amount already paid**, unless the attestation was knowingly false. **An amount not recovered by reason of this subsection gives rise to no debt, discharges no indebtedness, and has the same character for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 as the benefit as which it was paid.**\n\n**(d) No condition on the individual.**\u2014Neither the making of an attestation under subsection (b) nor its reconciliation under subsection (c) may be made a condition of eligibility, a ground for delay, or a basis for adverse action.\n\n**(e) Standing direction.**\u2014A direction given by an application to a covered program\u2014\n\n**(1)** continues while the individual is enrolled in, or an application of the individual is pending with, that program;\n\n**(2)** covers each furnishing to that program, including each furnishing a renewal or redetermination of the individual's eligibility requires;\n\n**(3)** lapses when the individual ceases both to be enrolled in the program and to have an application pending with it, and no furnishing to the program may be made on it thereafter; and\n\n**(4)** may be revoked by the individual at any time, in a form the Secretary shall prescribe, without reason. **Neither a revocation nor its absence may be treated as evidence of anything, and a revocation is not a ground for denying, reducing, delaying, or terminating a benefit** \u2014 an individual who has revoked establishes income as that individual would have done had this Act not been enacted.\n\nA direction given otherwise than by an application states its own scope and continues until revoked."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Disclosure of return information for income-tested benefit programs; individual objection and notice.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**The amendment \u2014 as the section will read.**\n\n**New language marked as inserted. Struck language marked as struck.**\n\n**(a) In general.**\u2014Section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\"(7) Disclosure of returns and return information to Federal, State, local, **and Tribal** agencies administering income-tested benefit programs, **subject to the objection of the individual****\n>\n> **\"(A) Disclosure by the Commissioner of Social Security.**\u2014The Commissioner of Social Security shall, ~~upon written request~~ **in accordance with subparagraph (F)**, disclose return information from returns with respect to net earnings from self-employment (as defined in section 1402), wages (as defined in section 3121(a) or 3401(a)), and payments of retirement income, which have been disclosed to the Social Security Administration as provided by paragraph (1) or (5) of this subsection, to any Federal, State, local, **or Tribal** agency administering a **covered** program ~~listed in subparagraph (D)~~ **described in subparagraph (D)**.\n>\n> **\"(B) Disclosure by the Secretary.**\u2014The Secretary shall, ~~upon written request~~ **in accordance with subparagraph (F)**, disclose current return information from returns with respect to unearned income from the Internal Revenue Service files to any Federal, State, local, **or Tribal** agency administering a **covered** program ~~listed in subparagraph (D)~~ **described in subparagraph (D)**.\n>\n> **\"(C) Purpose limitation.**\u2014The Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary shall disclose return information under subparagraphs (A) and (B) only for purposes of, and to the extent necessary in, determining eligibility for, or the correct amount of, benefits under a covered program. **An agency that would use return information disclosed under this paragraph for any other purpose does not have authority under this paragraph to receive it.**\n>\n> **\"(D) Covered program.**\u2014For purposes of this paragraph, the term 'covered program' means a program administered by a Federal, State, local, or Tribal agency **that conditions eligibility for, or the amount of, a benefit on the income of an individual** and that has elected in under section 3-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act. **A program listed in this subparagraph as in effect on the day before the date of enactment of that Act is a covered program without further election.**\n>\n> **\"(E) Objection.**\u2014An individual may at any time object to disclosure of return information concerning that individual under this paragraph, as to any or all covered programs.\n>\n> **\"(i)** An objection takes effect on receipt, applies to every disclosure thereafter, and **requires no reason and no approval by any person.**\n>\n> **\"(ii)** An individual who has objected may withdraw the objection at any time, and may object again thereafter. **Neither an objection nor its withdrawal may be treated as evidence of anything.**\n>\n> **\"(iii)** **An objection is not a ground for denying, reducing, delaying, or terminating a benefit.** An individual who has objected establishes income as that individual would have done **had the amendments made by section 2-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act not been made**.\n>\n> **\"(iv)** An objection is given to the Commissioner of Social Security or the Secretary and, on receipt, **binds both.** **The Commissioner and the Secretary shall each transmit to the other, on receipt, each objection and each withdrawal received. Transmission is a ministerial duty, and a failure to transmit does not limit the effect of the objection.**\n>\n> **\"(F) Recurring, application-triggered, and directed disclosure.**\u2014\n>\n> **\"(i)** Except with respect to an individual who has objected under subparagraph (E), the Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary shall make the disclosures described in subparagraphs (A) and (B) **not less often than every 2 business days (excluding Saturdays, Sundays, and legal public holidays), each disclosure covering the information received by the Commissioner or the Secretary since the preceding disclosure** \u2014 **and only with respect to an individual who is an applicant for or recipient of benefits under the covered program, whose eligibility the program is redetermining, or who has directed a furnishing to the program under that Act. This paragraph authorizes no disclosure concerning any other individual.**\n>\n> **\"(ii)** **On the making of an application by an individual to a covered program**, the Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary shall make the disclosures described in subparagraphs (A) and (B) with respect to that individual to the agency administering that program, **not later than 2 business days (excluding Saturdays, Sundays, and legal public holidays) after receiving notice of the application from that agency**, unless the individual has objected under subparagraph (E).\n>\n> **\"(iii)** **The Secretary shall furnish an individual's verified income record as section 1-6 of the Keep Your Benefits Act provides, and a furnishing under that section is a disclosure authorized by this paragraph.** **An objection under subparagraph (E) applies to the disclosures described in clauses (i) and (ii). It does not apply to a furnishing made on the direction of the individual, an application to a covered program being a direction as to that program under that Act.**\n>\n> **\"(iv)** No disclosure under this subparagraph shall be conditioned on a request by the receiving agency, and **each disclosure under this subparagraph is a ministerial duty.**\n>\n> **\"(G) Notice to the individual.**\u2014The Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary shall furnish to each individual whose return information is disclosed under this paragraph a statement identifying **what was disclosed, to which agency, on what date, and for which covered program**, together with **a plain statement of the right to object under subparagraph (E), of how to exercise it, and of its effect \u2014 including that an objection does not affect eligibility for any benefit and that an individual who objects establishes income by the means available before the objection right existed.** **Furnishing the statement is a ministerial duty**, is made for each calendar month in which such a disclosure was made, not later than the end of the following month, and **is not conditioned on any request.**\n>\n> **\"(H) Anti-substitution.**\u2014Where an agency obtains, or seeks to obtain, **substantially the same information concerning an individual under any other authority or from any other source, including a commercial source**, for a purpose described in subparagraph (C), **the conditions of this paragraph apply to that acquisition and use as well, and the acquiring agency bears the duties this paragraph places on the Commissioner of Social Security and the Secretary \u2014 the notice under subparagraph (G), effect for an objection under subparagraph (E), and the purpose limitation of subparagraph (C) \u2014 with respect to the information so acquired.** The agency shall publish a statement identifying the authority or source and every difference between its conditions and this paragraph's. **The omission of a purpose from this paragraph is a determination of Congress that the purpose is not authorized, and not a gap to be filled by regulation, agreement, or practice.**\"\n\n**(b) Conforming reference.**\u2014Any reference in law to a program listed in section 6103(l)(7)(D), **or to any subparagraph or clause of section 6103(l)(7) as in effect on the day before the date of enactment of this Act**, shall be treated as a reference to a covered program within the meaning of that subparagraph as amended by this section, **or to the corresponding provision of section 6103(l)(7) as so amended, with respect to the same program**.\n\n**(c) Safeguards preserved.**\u2014Section 6103(p)(4) continues to apply to each agency receiving return information under section 6103(l)(7) as amended by this section. **Nothing in this section limits section 7213, section 7213A, or section 7431.**\n\n**(d) Confidentiality conformed.**\u2014Section 6103(a)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by striking *\"or any local agency administering a program listed in subsection (l)(7)(D)\"* and inserting *\"or any local **or Tribal** agency administering a program **described in** subsection (l)(7)(D)\"*.\n\n> *As amended, \u00a7 6103(a)(2) will read:* \"\u2026no officer or employee of any State, any local law enforcement agency receiving information under subsection (i)(1)(C) or (7)(A), any tribal or local child support enforcement agency, **or any local or Tribal agency administering a program described in subsection (l)(7)(D)** who has or had access to returns or return information under this section or section 6104(c)\u2026\"","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Consolidation of benefit-administration access to the National Directory of New Hires.","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**The amendment \u2014 as the section will read.**\n\n**(a) In general.**\u2014Section 453(j) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 653(j)) is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** by striking paragraphs **(3)**, **(7)**, **(8)**, **(10)**, and **(11)**; and\n\n**(2)** by inserting after paragraph (2) the following:\n\n> **\"(3) Information comparisons and disclosure to assist in administration of income-tested benefit programs**\n>\n> **\"(A) In general.**\u2014The Secretary shall, on the direction of the individual to whom the information relates, disclose information in the National Directory of New Hires to a Federal, State, local, or Tribal agency administering a covered program, **for the purpose of, and only to the extent necessary in, determining eligibility for, or the correct amount of, a benefit under that program.**\n>\n> **\"(B) Covered program.**\u2014For purposes of this paragraph, the term 'covered program' means a program that conditions eligibility for, or the amount of, a benefit on the income of an individual, and that has elected in under section 3-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act. **A program administering a benefit described in the paragraphs struck by section 2-2 of that Act is a covered program without further election.**\n>\n> **\"(C) Purposes foreclosed.**\u2014**No information disclosed under this paragraph may be used, directly or indirectly, for any investigative, enforcement, collection, or immigration purpose.** **An agency that would use the information for such a purpose does not have authority under this paragraph to receive it**, and no other provision of law shall be construed to supply that authority for information obtained under this paragraph.\n>\n> **\"(D) Anti-substitution.**\u2014Where an agency obtains, or seeks to obtain, substantially the same information concerning an individual under any other authority, **the conditions of this paragraph apply to that authority as well**, and the agency shall publish a statement identifying both authorities and every difference between their conditions.\n>\n> **\"(E) No enlargement.**\u2014**This paragraph authorizes no disclosure that was not authorized by the paragraphs struck by section 2-2 of the Keep Your Benefits Act, except that it requires the direction of the individual and extends to programs described in subparagraph (B).** Nothing in this paragraph extends the period for which information is retained under subsection (i)(2)(A).\n>\n> **\"(F) Conditions on the receiving agency.**\u2014An agency receiving information under this paragraph\u2014\n>\n> **\"(i)** shall seek and receive information **only to the extent necessary** to verify the income of an individual for the purpose described in subparagraph (A);\n>\n> **\"(ii)** may use the information **only for that purpose**, except that it may conduct analyses of income reporting **after removal of personal identifiers**;\n>\n> **\"(iii)** may disclose the information onward **only** to a subordinate agency administering the same covered program, to its Inspector General, and to the Attorney General in connection with the administration of that program; and\n>\n> **\"(iv)** shall bear the costs of the comparison and disclosure, on such terms as the Secretary prescribes; and\n>\n> **\"(v)** shall not make information obtained under this paragraph available under section 552 of title 5, United States Code.\n>\n> **\"(G) Non-interference.**\u2014Where the Secretary determines that a disclosure under this paragraph would interfere with the effective operation of the program under this part, the Secretary may defer that disclosure for the period the determination states, **which shall not exceed 10 business days**, and shall publish the determination, the reasons for it, and the period of deferral. **A deferral may be renewed only by a new published determination.** A disclosure not deferred by a published determination **shall be made**, and an unpublished determination defers nothing.\n>\n> **\"(H) Operation of the comparison published.**\u2014**Subparagraph (F)(v) applies to information concerning an individual and does not apply to information concerning the operation of the comparison itself.** Each agency receiving information under this paragraph shall publish annually, in a form that identifies no individual\u2014\n>\n> **\"(i)** the number of comparisons performed;\n>\n> **\"(ii)** the number that resulted in a match;\n>\n> **\"(iii)** the number of matches that preceded a denial, reduction, suspension, or termination of a benefit; and\n>\n> **\"(iv)** the number of such actions subsequently reversed.\n>\n> **\"Publication under this subparagraph is a ministerial duty.**\n>\n> **\"(I) Disclosure for compilation of verified income records.**\u2014The Secretary shall disclose information in the National Directory of New Hires to the Secretary of the Treasury, **without any direction of, or any action by, an individual**, solely for the compilation of verified income records under section 1-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act. The Secretary of the Treasury\u2014\n>\n> **\"(i)** may use the information only for that compilation, and may disclose it onward only as that Act provides;\n>\n> **\"(ii)** shall bear the costs of the disclosure, on such terms as the Secretary prescribes; and\n>\n> **\"(iii)** shall not obtain under this subparagraph information deleted from the directory under subsection (i)(2)(A).\n>\n> **\"Subparagraphs (C), (F)(v), and (G) apply to disclosures under this subparagraph as they apply to disclosures to an agency administering a covered program.**\n>\n> **\"(J) Construction.**\u2014The omission of a purpose from this paragraph is a determination of Congress that the purpose is not authorized, and not a gap to be filled by regulation, agreement, or practice.\"\n\n**(b) Paragraphs preserved.**\u2014**Paragraphs (1), (2), (4), (5), (6), and (9) of section 453(j) are not amended by this Act.** **Nothing in this section consolidates, expands, or otherwise affects any authority relating to enforcement, collection, research, paternity establishment, or verification by the Social Security Administration.**\n\n**(c) Penalty preserved.**\u2014**Section 453(l)(2) of the Social Security Act continues to apply** to information disclosed under section 453(j)(3) as amended by this section.\n\n**(d) Conforming references.**\u2014Any reference in law to a paragraph struck by subsection (a)(1) shall be treated as a reference to section 453(j)(3) as amended by this section."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Election.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ELECTION OF COVERED PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) Who may elect.**\u2014The Federal, State, local, or Tribal agency administering a program that conditions eligibility for, or the amount of, a benefit on the income of an individual may elect that the program be a covered program under this Act.\n\n**(b) Form and contents.**\u2014An election is made by a writing to the Secretary that identifies\u2014\n\n**(1)** the program and the agency administering it;\n\n**(2)** the provision of Federal, State, local, or Tribal law establishing the program; and\n\n**(3)** the income test the program applies.\n\n**No other content is required, and the absence of a prescribed form defeats no election.**\n\n**(c) Effective on receipt.**\u2014An election takes effect on receipt by the Secretary. **Receipt is a fact, not a determination: no approval, finding, or other act of the Secretary or of any other officer is required, and none is authorized.** A program whose election has been received is a covered program for every purpose of this Act, including section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and section 453(j)(3) of the Social Security Act as amended by this Act.\n\n**(d) Withdrawal.**\u2014The electing agency may withdraw an election by a writing to the Secretary. A withdrawal takes effect at the start of the first full recompilation cycle under section 5-1(a) beginning after its receipt. **Withdrawal ends the program's receipt of records and disclosures under this Act; it does not affect the conditions that attached to anything already received, which survive withdrawal.**\n\n**(e) Programs covered without election.**\u2014A program described in section 6103(l)(7)(D) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 as in effect on the day before the date of enactment of this Act, or administering a benefit described in the paragraphs struck by section 2-2, **is a covered program without election**, and may not withdraw under subsection (d) except as the statute governing that program provides.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ELECTION OF COVERED PROGRAMS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Register of covered programs.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ELECTION OF COVERED PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) Publication.**\u2014The Secretary shall publish, and keep current, a register of covered programs, stating for each the contents of its election under section 3-1(b) or the basis of its coverage under section 3-1(e). **Publication is a ministerial duty.**\n\n**(b) Declaratory, not constitutive.**\u2014**The register records coverage; it does not create it.** A program whose election has been received is a covered program whether or not the register lists it, and an error or omission in the register changes no program's status and defeats no furnishing, disclosure, or duty under this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Voluntariness protected.","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ELECTION OF COVERED PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) No mandate.**\u2014Nothing in this Act requires any program to elect, and no consequence attaches under any provision of Federal law to a program's decision not to elect.\n\n**(b) Conditions attach to use, never to adoption.**\u2014The conditions of this Act, of section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and of section 453(j)(3) of the Social Security Act attach to the **receipt and use** of records and disclosures. **No agency of the United States may condition any payment, grant, approval, or participation on a program's election or non-election under this Act.**\n\n**(c) Election is not submission.**\u2014An election subjects the electing program to the conditions stated in this Act and in the provisions it amends, **and to nothing else.** An election is not consent to any oversight, requirement, or condition not stated in this Act, and no regulation, agreement, or practice may add one. **The omission of a condition from this Act is a determination of Congress that it is not imposed, and not a gap to be filled.**"},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Enrollment without further application.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Authority.**\u2014A covered program may determine that an individual is eligible, and may enroll the individual, **on the basis of the individual's verified income record and the composition facts stated in it**, without further application, interview, documentation, or other action by the individual, where the record and those facts, **under the program's own eligibility rules**, establish that the individual qualifies.\n\n**(b) Computed, not judged.**\u2014The determination under subsection (a) is made by applying the program's eligibility rules to the facts stated. **Where a fact the program's rules require is not stated in the record \u2014 including where the record states under section 1-3(a)(5) that information for a period is absent \u2014 this section confers no authority, and the program proceeds under its regular procedures.** Nothing in this section authorizes the program, the Secretary, or any other person to estimate, impute, or presume a fact not stated.\n\n**(c) One way.**\u2014**The record and the composition facts may establish that an individual qualifies. They may not be used under this section to determine that an individual does not qualify**, to deny an application, or to reduce, suspend, or terminate a benefit; section 1-4(c) governs any such use.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Direction to other programs.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Option in every application.**\u2014Each covered program shall afford an individual applying to it the option to direct, in the application, that the individual's verified income record be furnished to any other covered program the individual selects, for determination under section 4-1. **The option is not a condition of the application, and declining it may not be treated as evidence of anything.**\n\n**(b) Effect.**\u2014A direction under subsection (a) is a direction under section 0-3(d) as to each program selected, and section 1-6 \u2014 including the standing-direction rules of section 1-6(e) \u2014 applies to it."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Notice and declination.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Notice.**\u2014Before or at enrollment under section 4-1, the covered program shall furnish the individual a plain statement of **what the individual has been enrolled in, on what facts, at what benefit, and subject to what obligations, if any**, together with the right to decline under subsection (b) and how to exercise it.\n\n**(b) Declination.**\u2014An individual may decline an enrollment under this section, or end it, at any time, in any written form, without reason. **A declination takes effect on receipt, requires no approval, and may not be treated as evidence of anything.** An individual who declines is in the position the individual would have occupied had this section not been enacted.\n\n**(c) No adverse linkage.**\u2014Neither an enrollment under this section, nor a declination, may be used to reduce, suspend, terminate, or delay any other benefit of the individual, or to initiate any review of it."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Assignment where a choice is required.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":"**(a) Enrollment does not fail for want of a choice.**\u2014Where enrollment in a covered program requires a selection among plans, options, or providers and the individual has made none, the program shall enroll the individual and assign a selection **by a neutral method it has published in advance**.\n\n**(b) The assignment is provisional in the individual's hands.**\u2014An individual assigned under subsection (a) may change the selection at any time before, and for not less than the period set in the schedule after, the assignment takes effect, without penalty, fee, or waiting period; and thereafter as the program's rules provide for any enrollee. **An assignment is not evidence of a choice.**\n\n**(c) Where no method is published.**\u2014**A program that has not published an assignment method may not assign under subsection (a)** \u2014 it shall instead treat the individual as enrolled pending selection, at no cost to the individual, until a selection is made or a method is published."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Status of enrollment under this section.","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENROLLMENT ON THE RECORD","body":"An individual enrolled under this section has the same status, rights, benefits, and protections as an individual enrolled on application, **including the renewal protections of section 5**. Any reconciliation of a benefit determined under this section follows section 1-6(c), including its one-way rule."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Verification cycle.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":"**(a) Recompilation.**\u2014The Secretary shall recompile each verified income record on the cycle set in the schedule, which shall be **not less frequent than quarterly**.\n\n**(b) Verification, not accounting.**\u2014This section governs **how often eligibility is verified and by what means.** It does not change, and shall not be construed to change, **the period over which income is counted** for any covered program, or **who qualifies** for any benefit.\n\n**(c) One-way effect.**\u2014Where a recompilation or a report of changed circumstances would **increase** a benefit or **establish** eligibility, the covered program shall give it effect **as soon as the change is detected or reported**. Where it would **reduce or terminate** a benefit, the covered program shall give it effect **no earlier than the next scheduled redetermination**. **For purposes of this subsection, a change is detected by a covered program no later than the program's receipt of a recompiled verified income record, or of information disclosed under section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 or section 453(j)(3) of the Social Security Act, reflecting the change. A redetermination scheduled by reason of the change is not a scheduled redetermination for purposes of this subsection.**\n\n**(d) Construction.**\u2014Subsection (c) creates no entitlement to a benefit for which an individual is not otherwise eligible, and **does not extend a benefit beyond a period fixed by the statute governing the covered program.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Renewal without requiring information from the individual.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":"**(a) Escape hatches struck.**\u2014Section 1413(c)(3) of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (42 U.S.C. 18083(c)(3)) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\"(3) Determination of eligibility on available data**\n>\n> **\"(A) In general.**\u2014Each applicable State health subsidy program shall ~~, to the maximum extent practicable,~~\u2014\n>\n> **\"(i)** establish, verify, and update eligibility for participation in the program using the data matching arrangement under paragraph (2); and\n>\n> **\"(ii)** determine such eligibility on the basis of reliable, third party data.\n>\n> **\"(B)** ~~This paragraph shall not apply in circumstances with respect to which the Secretary determines that the administrative and other costs of use of the data matching arrangement under paragraph (2) outweigh its expected gains in accuracy, efficiency, and program participation.~~ **Where a program cannot establish, verify, or update eligibility under subparagraph (A) with respect to an individual, it shall proceed under sections 5-2(f) and 5-2(g) of the Keep Your Benefits Act. No officer may disapply this paragraph, in whole or as to any class of individuals, by a determination as to cost.**\"\n\n**(b) Duty extended to every covered program.**\u2014Before requesting any information from an individual in connection with a renewal or redetermination of eligibility, **a covered program** shall determine whether eligibility can be renewed on information already available to it, and shall renew on that information where it can. **This subsection applies whether or not the covered program is an applicable State health subsidy program within the meaning of section 1413(e) of that Act.**\n\n**(c) Sources that must be examined.**\u2014In making the determination under subsection (b), the covered program shall examine, at a minimum\u2014\n\n**(1)** the verified income record compiled under section 1-1;\n\n**(2)** information disclosed to it under section 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and section 453(j)(3) of the Social Security Act;\n\n**(3)** information already contained in the individual's own account or case file; and\n\n**(4)** any other information the covered program holds or has a present right to obtain.\n\n**(d) Individual determination.**\u2014The determination under subsection (b) shall be made **as to each individual.** A covered program **may not decline to renew an individual on available information because information concerning another member of that individual's household is unavailable, incomplete, or unverified.**\n\n**(e) No interview.**\u2014A covered program shall not require an in-person interview as a condition of renewal.\n\n**(f) Record of inability.**\u2014Where a covered program does not renew under subsection (b), it shall record, in the individual's case file, **which sources under subsection (c) it examined and what information was missing.** The record shall be furnished to the individual with any request for information.\n\n**(g) Time to respond.**\u2014Where a covered program requests information from an individual, it shall allow **not less than the period set in the schedule**, which shall be **not less than 30 days** from the date of the request.\n\n**(h) Operation published.**\u2014Each covered program shall publish annually, **in a form that identifies no individual**\u2014\n\n**(1)** the number of renewals completed under subsection (b) without a request to the individual;\n\n**(2)** the number completed after a request;\n\n**(3)** the number of terminations for failure to respond; and\n\n**(4)** the number of such terminations reversed on reconsideration.\n\n**Publication under this subsection is a ministerial duty.**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Continuation of eligibility where the program has not acted.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":"**(a) Continuation.**\u2014Where a covered program has not completed a renewal by the date eligibility would otherwise end, and **the information available to it does not establish that the individual is ineligible**, the individual's eligibility **continues by operation of this subsection** until the covered program completes the renewal. **No further act by any person is required.**\n\n**(b) No procedural termination against the record.**\u2014A covered program **shall not terminate, reduce, or suspend a benefit for failure to return a form or to supply information where the information already available to it establishes that the individual remains eligible.**\n\n**(c) Effect of a failure to record.**\u2014Where a covered program has not made the record required by section 5-2(f), it **has not made the determination required by section 5-2(b)**, and subsection (a) applies."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Reconsideration after termination for failure to respond.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":"**(a) Reconsideration.**\u2014Where an individual is terminated for failure to return a renewal form or to supply requested information, the covered program shall reconsider eligibility, **without requiring a new application**, if the individual supplies the form or information within **the period set in the schedule**, which shall be **not less than 90 days** after the date of termination. **The notice of termination shall state the reconsideration period, what must be supplied, and that no new application is required.**\n\n**(b) Retroactivity.**\u2014Where reconsideration establishes that the individual was eligible throughout, **eligibility is reinstated retroactive to the date of termination**, and the covered program shall treat the intervening period as one of continuous eligibility.\n\n**(c) Longer periods preserved.**\u2014A State or a covered program may allow a period longer than the period under subsection (a). **It may not allow a shorter one.**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. Relation to other law.","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 THE VERIFICATION CYCLE","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.**\u2014Sections 5-2 through 5-4 establish **minimum** requirements. A regulation, State plan, or waiver **may extend a period, add a source that must be examined, or afford greater protection to an individual. It may not shorten a period, remove a source, or reduce a protection below the level set by those sections.**\n\n**(b) Existing regulation preserved and unsunsetted.**\u2014Nothing in this Act limits 42 CFR \u00a7 435.916 as in effect on the day before the date of enactment. **Subsection (h) of that section, and any other provision terminating its effect by lapse of time, shall have no force**, and the requirements of that section shall continue in effect as minimum requirements without regard to any sunset date.\n\n**(c) Independent duty.**\u2014The requirements of sections 5-2 through 5-4 **relieve no covered program of any duty it owes under any other provision of law.**\n\n**(d) Relation to State participation.**\u2014As applied to a program administered by a State, local, or Tribal agency, sections 5-2 through 5-4 are **conditions on the program's participation in the Federal program it administers and on its receipt of records and disclosures under this Act**, enforceable as such conditions. **Nothing in this Act commands a State, local, or Tribal officer to administer a Federal regulatory program**, and no consequence of noncompliance with those sections extends to any Federal payment other than as the compliance provision of this Act specifically provides."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Proposed return furnished.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"**(a) Duty.**\u2014For each taxable year, the Secretary shall prepare and furnish to each individual described in subsection (b) a **proposed return** \u2014 a return of Federal income tax for the individual for that year, completed from the information described in section 1-2, stating the tax or refund it computes, **including each credit the stated facts support**.\n\n**(b) Covered individuals.**\u2014Subsection (a) applies to an individual whose items of gross income for the taxable year, as shown in the information described in section 1-2, consist of items reported on information returns or in quarterly wage data, **whether or not the individual is required to file and whether or not the individual has ever filed.** The Secretary may extend subsection (a) to further classes of individuals; **the Secretary may not narrow the class stated in this subsection.**\n\n**(c) When.**\u2014A proposed return shall be furnished not later than the date set in the schedule, which shall fall before the date prescribed for filing returns for the taxable year.\n\n**(d) Unverifiable facts identified.**\u2014A proposed return shall identify, conspicuously and in plain language, **each fact it relies on that the information described in section 1-2 cannot establish** \u2014 including the residency and relationship of an individual proposed as a qualifying child \u2014 and shall state that the individual must **confirm or correct** each such fact before the proposed return may be accepted.\n\n**(e) Plain statement.**\u2014A proposed return shall state, conspicuously and in plain language, that it is a proposal; that the individual may accept it, correct it, or disregard it; and that disregarding it has no consequence, as section 6-2(c) provides.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Effect of the proposal.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"**(a) Acceptance.**\u2014An individual who accepts a proposed return \u2014 confirming each fact identified under section 6-1(d), by a means that verifies the individual's identity under section 6-4 \u2014 **has filed a return of Federal income tax** for the taxable year, with the same effect in all respects as a return prepared and filed by the individual.\n\n**(b) Correction.**\u2014An individual may accept a proposed return as corrected by the individual, and subsection (a) applies to the return as corrected.\n\n**(c) Disregard.**\u2014**A proposed return that is not accepted has no effect.** It is not a return of the individual; it commences no examination and supports no assessment, penalty, addition to tax, or inference; and the individual's obligations and elections under this title are what they would have been had it never been furnished. **Failure to respond to a proposed return is not a failure to file, and shall not be treated as evidence of one.**\n\n**(d) The proposal binds no one.**\u2014A proposed return is not a determination, an assessment, or an examination. Its contents carry no presumption of correctness against the individual, **and an individual who files a return differing from the proposal is not, by reason of the difference, subject to examination, penalty, or any adverse inference.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. The earned income tax credit.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"**(a) Proposed, not asserted.**\u2014A proposed return shall state each credit under section 32 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 that the facts stated in the record and the composition facts support, computed on those facts \u2014 **subject to section 6-1(d): a fact the Government cannot establish is proposed for confirmation, never asserted.**\n\n**(b) Effect of confirmation.**\u2014A credit claimed by acceptance under section 6-2(a) is claimed by the individual on the individual's return, with the same effect, and subject to the same provisions of law, as a credit claimed on any other return. **Nothing in this section modifies section 32, and nothing in this section is a determination by the Secretary that any individual is entitled to any credit.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Identity.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"Acceptance under section 6-2(a) is valid only by a means that verifies the identity of the individual, which the Secretary shall prescribe. **Until prescribed, the means of signature and authentication accepted for returns generally suffice, and the absence of a prescribed means defeats no acceptance made by them.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Duty not to be contracted away.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"The Secretary may not enter into, renew, or enforce any agreement that restricts the performance of this section or the Secretary's right to prepare, propose, or receive returns under it. **Any term of an agreement inconsistent with this section is void as against public policy to the extent of the inconsistency.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6-6","heading":"\u00a7 6-6. Relation to existing law.","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE PROPOSED RETURN","body":"**(a) Section 6014 preserved.**\u2014The election under section 6014 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 remains available, and this section operates whether or not it is made.\n\n**(b) Filing preserved.**\u2014Nothing in this section limits any means of preparing or filing a return otherwise available to any individual, or requires any individual to use a proposed return for any purpose."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Permanent appropriation.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, **without fiscal year limitation**, such sums as are necessary to carry out sections 1-1, 1-5, 1-6, 3-2, 5-1, and 6-1 of this Act, and the disclosure, notice, transmission, and publication duties of sections 6103(l)(7) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and 453(j)(3) of the Social Security Act as amended by this Act.\n\n**(b) Character.**\u2014Amounts under subsection (a) are available until expended and are in addition to amounts otherwise available. **No apportionment, deferral, or administrative subdivision of amounts under subsection (a) may have the effect of suspending, delaying, or degrading a duty named in it.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Continuity.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"A lapse in discretionary appropriations does not suspend, defer, or excuse any duty under this Act or under a provision amended by it. **The functions named in section 7-1(a) continue during any such lapse, funded by that section**, and no officer may direct otherwise."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Acceptance satisfies Federal verification.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014Where a covered program determines eligibility for, or the amount of, a benefit on the basis of a verified income record, **that determination satisfies every requirement of Federal law that the program verify the income of the individual for that determination**, including\u2014\n\n**(1)** section 1320b-7 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1320b-7), as to income;\n\n**(2)** section 1411 of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (42 U.S.C. 18081), as to household income;\n\n**(3)** any income verification requirement applicable to needs-based benefits administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; and\n\n**(4)** any income review requirement applicable to housing assistance programs administered by the Secretary of Housing and Urban Development.\n\n**(b) Income only, and granting only.**\u2014Subsection (a) reaches verification of **income**. It does not satisfy, relieve, or modify any requirement to verify citizenship, immigration status, residency, identity, or any other non-income element. **And it reaches only verification required to establish or grant a benefit: it does not satisfy, relieve, or modify any requirement to verify information before a benefit is denied, reduced, suspended, or terminated \u2014 including section 5317(b) of title 38, United States Code \u2014 every such requirement remaining in full force.**\n\n**(c) Floor, not ceiling.**\u2014Subsection (a) does not require any program to rely on a verified income record, and does not limit section 1-4(b)."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Income and eligibility verification systems conformed.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Section 1320b-7(a)(2) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1320b-7(a)(2)) is amended\u2014\n\n**(1)** by inserting after *\"available pursuant to section 6103(l)(7) of such Code,\"* the following: **\"information received under subparagraph (F) of that section, and the verified income record furnished under the Keep Your Benefits Act,\"**; and\n\n**(2)** by adding at the end the following: **\"Information received under section 6103(l)(7)(F) of such Code, and a verified income record furnished under the Keep Your Benefits Act, shall be utilized whether or not requested, and section 5-2 of that Act governs their examination; a determination as to usefulness under this paragraph does not limit that duty;\"**."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Severability.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014Each provision of this Act, and each application of each provision, is severable. If any provision or application is held invalid, the remainder of this Act, and the application of the provision to any other person or circumstance, is unaffected.\n\n**(b) No integrated-scheme inference.**\u2014**A court shall not treat this Act as a single integrated scheme whose provisions stand or fall together.** Each section states machinery that operates independently: the record (section 1) without the disclosures (section 2); the disclosures without the enrollment authority (section 4); the verification cycle (section 5) without any of them, on the sources a program otherwise holds.\n\n**(c) One exception, stated.**\u2014**Sections 7-3 and 7-4 are severable from this Act only together**, being one set of enabling amendments; the invalidity of either does not affect any other section."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Effective dates.","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) In general.**\u2014This Act takes effect on the date of its enactment.\n\n**(b) First compilation.**\u2014The first verified income records shall be compiled not later than the date set in the schedule.\n\n**(c) First proposed returns.**\u2014Section 6 applies to taxable years beginning after the date set in the schedule.\n\n**(d) Amendments.**\u2014The amendments made by this Act take effect on the date of enactment, and the first disclosures under section 6103(l)(7)(F) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 shall be made not later than the first cycle following the date under subsection (b)."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"S-1 to S-4 are stated in the amendatory text of section 2, which the United States Code will carry; the rows record them for the drafting committee.\n\n| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 2-1(a), new \u00a7 6103(l)(7)(F)(i) recurring disclosure cycle | Not less often than every 2 business days, each disclosure covering the information received since the preceding disclosure (stated in the amendment) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 2-1(a), new \u00a7 6103(l)(7)(F)(ii) application-triggered disclosure period | 2 business days after the Commissioner or the Secretary receives notice of the application (stated in the amendment) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 2-1(a), new \u00a7 6103(l)(7)(G) notice cycle | One statement for each calendar month in which a disclosure concerning the individual was made, furnished not later than the end of the following month (stated in the amendment) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 2-2(a), new \u00a7 453(j)(3)(G) maximum deferral period | 10 business days (stated in the amendment) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 5-1(a) recompilation cycle | Within 2 business days after the Secretary receives information described in section 1-2(a) concerning the individual, and not less often than quarterly | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 5-2(g) response period | 90 days from the date of the request | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 5-4(a) reconsideration period | 90 days after the date of termination | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 4-4(b) post-assignment change period | 90 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 6-1(c) proposed-return furnishing date | March 1 following the taxable year, for an individual whose items of gross income for the year are all reported on returns or statements due on or before January 31 under section 6071(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, or in quarterly wage data; April 1 following the taxable year for any other individual | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 7-6(b) first-compilation date | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 7-6(c) date after which a taxable year beginning is a first proposed-return year | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 1-6(a), (b) period to furnish a verified income record on an individual's direction; on expiry the covered program accepts the individual's attestation under \u00a7 1-6(b) | 2 business days after the direction is given | *(Reserved.)* |\n\nIn this schedule, **business day** means a day other than a Saturday, a Sunday, a legal public holiday under section 6103(a) of title 5, United States Code, or a day on which such a holiday is observed by Federal employees whose basic workweek is Monday through Friday."}],"asOf":"2026-11-12"}}}};
